The '''2015 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League]], the 44th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the sixth season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at the [[National Stadium, Warsaw|National Stadium]] in [[Warsaw]], Poland, on 27 May 2015,<ref name="access">{{cite web |title=2014/15 calendar and access list |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/season=2015/accesslist/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140527215715/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/season=2015/accesslist/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=27 May 2014 |publisher=UEFA |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> between Ukrainian side [[FC Dnipro|Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk]] and the title holders, Spanish side [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]]. Sevilla won the match 3–2 for a record fourth title.<ref>{{cite news |first=Jonathan |last=Wilson |title=Carlos Bacca double breaks Dnipro hearts for Sevilla to make history |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/may/27/dnipro-dnipropetrovsk-sevilla-europa-league-final-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=28 May 2015 |access-date=28 May 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Gary |last=Rose |title=Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk 2-3 Sevilla |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/32908634 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=28 May 2015 |access-date=28 May 2015 }}</ref>

As winners, Sevilla earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]], [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], in the [[2015 UEFA Super Cup]]. Moreover, for the first time, a place in the UEFA Champions League was reserved for the UEFA Europa League winners, meaning that Sevilla automatically qualified for the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League]] despite not qualifying through their domestic league position.<ref>{{cite web |title=New approach broadens Europa League appeal |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0257-0def7f60e195-fd6dc0500321-1000--new-approach-broadens-europa-league-appeal/ |publisher=UEFA |date=29 August 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:2015 UEFA Europa League Final.jpg|thumb|The National Stadium in Warsaw, Poland, hosted the final.|211x211px]]
The [[National Stadium, Warsaw|National Stadium]] was announced as the venue of the final at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting on 23 May 2013.<ref>{{cite web |first=Mark |last=Chaplin |title=Executive Committee decides hosts for 2015 finals |url=http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/executive-committee/news/newsid=1954884.html |publisher=UEFA |date=23 May 2013 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> This was the first UEFA club final hosted in Poland.

The National Stadium is a retractable roof football stadium located in Warsaw, Poland. It is used mostly for football matches and it is the home stadium of [[Poland national football team]]. The stadium has a seating capacity of 58,145 which makes it the largest association football arena in Poland. Its construction started in 2008 and finished in November 2011. It is located on the site of the former [[10th-Anniversary Stadium|Stadion Dziesięciolecia]], on Aleja Zieleniecka in Praga Południe district, near the city centre. It hosted three group matches (including the opening match), a quarter-final, and a semi-final in [[UEFA Euro 2012]], co-hosted by Poland and Ukraine.

==Background==
This was Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk's first European final. They were the second Ukrainian team to reach the UEFA Cup/Europa League final, after [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]], who defeated [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] in the [[2009 UEFA Cup final|2009 final]] in [[Istanbul]], and the third Ukrainian team to play a European final, after Shakhtar Donetsk and [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kyiv]], who won two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals in [[1975 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1975]] and [[1986 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1986]] as representatives of the [[Soviet Union]]. Before this final, Ukrainian clubs had won every major European final which they had competed.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ukraine's flawless record in UEFA club finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0257-0defa0fee86a-9806927f76d2-1000--ukraine-s-flawless-record-in-uefa-club-finals/ |publisher=UEFA |date=21 May 2015 |access-date=28 May 2015 }}</ref>

This was Sevilla's fourth UEFA Cup/Europa League final. They won all three of their previous finals (in [[2006 UEFA Cup final|2006]], [[2007 UEFA Cup final|2007]] and [[2014 UEFA Europa League final|2014]]). As they won their fourth title, they became the outright record holder, breaking a tie with [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], [[Inter Milan]] and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Sevilla target UEFA Europa League history |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d817c7c9a5e-5cb61ff697a3-1000--sevilla-s-first-four-final-successes/ |publisher=UEFA |date=14 May 2015 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> Their manager [[Unai Emery]], who was also the winning manager in 2014, became the [[List of UEFA Cup and Europa League winning managers#Managers with multiple titles|fifth coach to win the title twice or more]], after [[Giovanni Trapattoni]] (Juventus in 1977 and 1993, Inter Milan in 1991), [[Luis Molowny]] ([[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in 1985 and 1986), [[Juande Ramos]] (Sevilla in 2006 and 2007) and [[Rafael Benítez]] ([[Valencia CF|Valencia]] in 2004, [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in 2013).<ref>{{cite web |title=Dnipro to face Sevilla in UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0252-0cdbb2ed1fd3-812d757b5bf1-1000--dnipro-to-face-sevilla-in-uefa-europa-league-final/ |publisher=UEFA |date=14 May 2015 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

The two sides had never met in UEFA club competitions.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2015/E/E_2015228_PK.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200104041437/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2015/E/E_2015228_PK.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=4 January 2020|title= 2015 UEFA Europa League Final Press Kit|date=27 May 2015|publisher=UEFA|access-date=27 May 2015}}</ref>

