===Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk===
As runners-up of the [[2013–14 Ukrainian Premier League]] behind Shakhtar, Dnipro were awarded a spot in the third qualifying round of the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]] for the first time in their history. They were drawn against Danish runners-up [[FC Copenhagen]], but a draw in the first leg at home, followed by a 2–0 defeat in Denmark, meant they dropped down into the Europa League play-off, where they defeated [[HNK Hajduk Split|Hajduk Split]] to reach the group stage for the third time in a row.

Dnipro were drawn in [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League group stage#Group F|Group F]] with [[Inter Milan]], [[AS Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]], and [[Qarabağ FK|Qarabağ]]. The group stage campaign started disastrously for the Ukrainians, who earned only a single point in their first three games with a 0–0 draw away to Saint-Étienne and 1–0 home defeats to both Inter and Qarabağ. On matchday 4, a 2–1 away win over the Azerbaijani team ended a winless run, but Dnipro were again defeated by Inter 2–1 in Milan, despite taking a 1–0 lead through [[Ruslan Rotan]]'s early goal. Dnipro went into their final group match at home to Saint-Étienne at the bottom of the Group F table and needing a win to stand a chance of qualifying;<ref>{{cite news |title=Europa League matchday six permutations |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d0424703171-5bdbbb9c21a9-1000--europa-league-matchday-six-permutations/ |publisher=UEFA |date=10 December 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> [[Artem Fedetskyi]] scored the only goal of the match midway through the second half to secure a 1–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |title=Fedetskiy takes Dnipro through |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/025a-0ea6f6d84d13-e4483d156081-1000--fedetskiy-takes-dnipro-through/ |publisher=UEFA |date=11 December 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> With Qarabağ only managing a 0–0 draw against group winners Inter, Dnipro finished the group stage in second place with seven points.<ref>{{cite news |title=Qarabağ hold Inter but fall short |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/025a-0ea6f65eb0d0-11ee8ee72f85-1000--qarabag-hold-inter-but-fall-short/ |publisher=UEFA |date=11 December 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

===Sevilla===
As title holders, Sevilla qualified for the group stage automatically, and were placed in Group G with [[Feyenoord]], [[HNK Rijeka|Rijeka]] and [[Standard Liège]]. They began their campaign with a 2–0 win over Feyenoord, with first-half goals from [[Grzegorz Krychowiak]] and [[Stéphane Mbia]]. Two weeks later, Mbia earned a 2–2 draw at Rijeka. Sevilla then played Liège twice, a goalless away draw and a 3–1 home victory with goals from [[Kevin Gameiro]], [[José Antonio Reyes]] and [[Carlos Bacca]]. They then lost 2–0 away to Feyenoord, sending the Dutch side to the knockout stage ahead of Sevilla. The Spanish side secured second place in the final game with a home victory over Rijeka via [[Denis Suárez]]'s first European goal.<ref>{{cite news |first=Nick |last=Aitken |title=Suárez sends Sevilla through at Rijeka's expense |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/021c-0e8eac10a2e1-132f7ef452b3-1000--suarez-sends-sevilla-through-at-rijeka-s-expense/ |publisher=UEFA |date=11 December 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
===Ambassador===
[[File:Jerzy_Dudek.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Jerzy Dudek]] was named as the ambassador for the final.]]
Former Poland international goalkeeper [[Jerzy Dudek]], who won the Champions League with [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in 2005, was named the ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dudek named Europa League final ambassador |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0257-0def7f8d1940-716ce2606c3b-1000--dudek-named-europa-league-final-ambassador/ |publisher=UEFA |date=29 August 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

===Officials===
In May 2015, English [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Martin Atkinson]] was chosen to oversee the final. He was joined by compatriots Stephen Child and Michael Mullarkey as [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], [[Andre Marriner]] and [[Anthony Taylor (referee)|Anthony Taylor]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Additional assistant referee|additional assistant referees]], Jake Collin as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official and reserve assistant referee|reserve assistant referee]], and the Czech [[Pavel Královec]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].<ref name="referee">{{cite news |title=Atkinson to referee UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0221-0e90c6cebc4d-789c81c9956e-1000--atkinson-to-referee-uefa-europa-league-final/ |publisher=UEFA |date=18 May 2015 |access-date=18 May 2015 }}</ref>

===Identity===
UEFA unveiled the visual identity of the final on 29 August 2014.<ref>{{cite web |title=Event design unveiled for 2015 UEFA Europa League final |url=http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/administration/marketing/news/newsid=2137071.html |publisher=UEFA |date=29 August 2014 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 56,000, a total of 44,000 tickets were made available to fans and the general public, with the two finalist teams receiving 9,500 tickets each and 25,000 tickets sold to fans worldwide via the UEFA website from 26 February to 25 March 2015 in four price categories: [[Euro|€]]130, €90, €65, and €40.<ref>{{cite web |title=Warsaw final tickets go on international sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/021e-0e9ac0759b50-c58c489691ab-1000--warsaw-final-tickets-go-on-international-sale/ |publisher=UEFA |date=26 February 2015 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

===Kits===
At the behest of club president José Castro, Sevilla chose to wear their red away kit, featuring their badge rather than the "SFC" monogram which they had previously been sporting.<ref>{{cite news|title=A la final de Varsovia, de color rojo y con el escudo actual|trans-title=At the final in Warsaw, in red and with the current badge|url=http://www.estadiodeportivo.com/sevilla/2015/05/18/final-varsovia-color-rojo-escudo-actual/41938.html|access-date=20 May 2015|work=Estadio Deportivo|date=18 May 2015|language=es}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Carlos_Bacca,_2012-07-17.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Carlos Bacca]] scored twice for Sevilla, including the winning goal]]
Although Sevilla dominated possession in the early exchanges, Dnipro opened the scoring after seven minutes when [[Nikola Kalinić (footballer)|Nikola Kalinić]] headed in a cross from [[Matheus (footballer, born 1983)|Matheus]]. In the 28th minute, Dnipro failed to clear from a free kick and Sevilla midfielder [[Grzegorz Krychowiak]] took possession of the ball, taking a step to evade [[Léo Matos]] before shooting an equaliser in his home country. Only three minutes later, the Spanish club took the lead, when captain [[José Antonio Reyes]], in what was reported to be his final match for Sevilla, found [[Carlos Bacca]] who rounded Dnipro goalkeeper [[Denys Boyko]]. Only two minutes before half time, Dnipro equalised, with [[Ruslan Rotan]] dispatching a free kick past [[Sergio Rico]].<ref name=guard>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Doyle |title=Dnipro 2–3 Sevilla |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2015/may/27/dnipro-v-sevilla-europa-league-final-live |work=The Guardian |date=27 May 2015 |access-date=28 May 2015 }}</ref>

In the 58th minute, Sevilla made the game's first substitution: Reyes made way for [[Coke (footballer)|Coke]], who went into his habitual right-back position, pushing [[Aleix Vidal]] forward into the right-wing position held by Reyes. Fifteen minutes later, Sevilla scored the winner, Bacca converting after being supplied by [[Vitolo (footballer, born 1989)|Vitolo]]. Soon after, Dnipro made an attacking change, replacing Kalinić with [[Yevhen Seleznyov]], and Sevilla substituted Bacca for [[Kevin Gameiro]]. The two teams then made their last substitutions in the closing stages, Dnipro replaced [[Jaba Kankava]] with [[Yevhen Shakhov (footballer, born 1990)|Yevhen Shakhov]] and Sevilla took off [[Éver Banega]] for [[Vicente Iborra]].<ref name=guard/>

Soon after all changes had been made, Dnipro's Matheus collapsed and was taken off by medical staff. Manager [[Myron Markevych]] confirmed that he was treated at hospital for a nasal fracture and a head injury, being discharged in good health hours later to reunite with his teammates.<ref>{{cite news |first=Luke |last=Meehan |title=Matheus fine and well after collapse – Markevych |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/166/europa-league/2015/05/28/12164792/matheus-fine-and-well-after-collapse-markevych |publisher=Goal |date=28 May 2015 |access-date=29 May 2015 }}</ref><!-- it would be nice if we could come up with a better (and more reliable) source than Goal.com -->

Sevilla's record fourth title meant that Spain and Italy were tied with the most UEFA Cup/Europa League titles, with both countries having won nine times.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0221-0e90e477a0d6-7757fcd8b605-1000--sevilla-out-on-their-own-in-all-time-standings/|title=Sevilla out on their own in all-time standings|publisher=UEFA|date=27 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c51090ec052-18f1e70610e7-1000--spain-join-italy-at-top-of-all-time-rankings/|title=Spain join Italy at top of all-time rankings|publisher=UEFA|date=27 May 2015}}</ref> [[José Antonio Reyes]] became the first player to win the UEFA Cup/Europa League four times, as he was also a member of the winning side for [[Atlético Madrid]] in 2010 and 2012 (although he missed the latter final) and for Sevilla in 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0221-0e90e8bc7e29-8ca1d6a5f787-1000--fantastic-four-for-sevilla-s-reyes/|title=Fantastic four for Sevilla's Reyes|publisher=UEFA|date=28 May 2015}}</ref> Three players had won the title three times: [[Ray Clemence]], [[Giuseppe Bergomi]], and [[Nicola Berti]].

